maxTol = 0.001;
tspan = [795 805];
%[text] **STEP** **2 Define** **the** **function** **and** **derivative**
%[text] - Use `syms` `x` to create symbolic variable.
%[text] - Define the function as `P(x)`.
%[text] - Use `diff` to calculate the derivative. \
syms x;
P(x) = YOUR FUNCTION;
dPx(x) = diff(P, x);
%[text] **STEP** **3 Convert** **functions** **to** **numerical** **functions**
%[text] Always use these two lines:
P = matlabFunction(P);
dPx = matlabFunction(dPx);
%[text] **STEP** **4:** **Plot** **the** **function**
%[text] - X-axis = `x`
%[text] - Y-axis = `f(x)` (your function)
%[text] - Plot over the interval given in the question.
%[text] - Add horizontal line at zero to see where the root is. \
figure;
fplot(P, tspan, 'linewidth', 2);
xlabel('x'); ylabel('f(x)');
yline(0, 'LineWidth', 2, 'Color', 'k');
grid on;
hold on;
%[text] **STEP** **5:** **Start** **Newton's** **Method**
%[text] - Set iteration counter to zero.
%[text] - Create a `while` loop.
%[text] - Continue until tolerance is reached OR 20 iterations have passed. \
itr = 0;
while abs(P(xr)) > maxTol && itr < 20

%[text] **STEP** **6:** **Update** **the** **root** **using** **Newton's** **formula**
%[text] Use this formula to calculate the next estimate:
xr = xr - P(xr) / dPx(xr);

fprintf('%4d     %8.4f   %10.4f\n', itr, xr, P(xr));
itr = itr + 1;
%[text] **STEP** **7:** **Finish** **the** **graph** **and** **display** **the** **root**
%[text] After the `end` of the while loop:
plot(xr, P(xr), 'ro', 'MarkerSize', 10);
title(sprintf('Root found at x = %.4f', xr));
xlim([795 805])
hold off;
